          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Lattice gauge theory
            (introduced in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wegner71"} Wegner 71
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Wilson74"} Wilson 74
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            , such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            is replaced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+%28in+a+vector+space%2C+etc.%29"} lattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+model"} lattice model
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field+theory"} gauge field theory
          %p
            Usually this is considered after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"} Wick rotation
            from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{3,1}
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+field+theory"} Euclidean field theory
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+%28in+a+vector+space%2C+etc.%29"} lattice
            inside
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3 \times S^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean"} Euclidean
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field+theory"} gauge field theory
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+%28in+a+vector+space%2C+etc.%29"} lattice
            inside the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-torus"} 4-torus
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi T
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^4
          %p
            This discretization and further
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} compactification
            has the effect that the would-be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            of the theory becomes an ordinary
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
          %p
            This allows us to consider (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"}> Wick-rotated
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral+quantization"} path integral quantization
            at fixed lattice spacing, this being, in principle, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            in terms of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            over the lattice correspond to the
            = succeed "-freedom" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %p
            Hence lattice gauge theory lends itself to brute-force simulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            on electronic computers, and the term is often understood by default in this sense. See
            %a{:href => "#FodorHoelbling12"} Fodor-Hoelbling 12
            for a good account.
          %p
            Since the explicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theories"} Yang-Mills theories
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            is presently wide open (see the references at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
            and at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton+sea"} instanton sea
            (
            %a{:href => "#Gruber13"}> Gruber 13
            ) and notably the phenomenon of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
            and explicit computation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/masses"} masses
            (
            %a{:href => "#Durr09"}> Durr et al. 09
            , see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FodorHoelbling12"} Fodor-Hoelbling 12, section V
          %p
            Despite the word “theory”, lattice gauge theory is more like “computer-simulated
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            = succeed "-problems" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+zeta-function"} Riemann zeta-function
            is to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+hypothesis"} Riemann hypothesis
            (see
            = succeed "))." do
              %a{:href => "#Riemann+hypothesis#ReferencesComputerChecks"} there
